   "url": "https://theosismedical.com/hacks/fast-food",
   "title": "Fast Food Hacks",
   "kind": "hack",
   "text": "06\nFast Food Hacks\nLet’s be honest, you are going to eat out. The frequency in which you do is directly correlated to the number of children under your care. Trust me, it’s science.\nI judge not. Plenty a meal has been enjoyed under the golden arches of calorically dense shame. A busy work week, a road trip, scrambling after the kid’s practice, or just no energy to cook — this is where you can survive derailment. The move is not “never drive through.” The move is to pick the highest protein-to-energy version of whatever joint you are already in.\nI went looking at published nutrition facts — official pages where they exist, and common tracker listings (MyFitnessPal / Carb Manager / FatSecret) for the no-bun and extra-meat versions. Menus move. Portioning is sloppy. If the numbers are on the screen, run × 10 yourself.\nThe list is not exhaustive. We focused on the low-hanging fruit — of the foods far from the health status of fruit — to hack our way to health in the drive-through. Lose the buns, find it in your soul to somehow ignore the fries, double the meat, grab a Coke Zero, and voilà… you’ve won fast food.\nP:E ≥ 2P:E ≥ 1P:E ≥ 0.5P:E < 0.5\nP:E is protein grams divided by carb grams plus fat grams. Green is more parts than fuel. Red is the opposite. What do we mean by “energy”?\n01 of 16\nChick-fil-A\nThe grilled nuggets are the cheat code. Fried nuggets are a different food.\nOrderCalP / C / F% proteinP:E\n8-ct grilled nuggets\nNo sauce, or Buffalo / mustard if you need one.\nTheir nutrition page13025 / 1 / 377%6.3\n12-ct grilled nuggets\nSame bird, more of it.\nTheir nutrition page20038 / 2 / 576%5.4\n30-ct grilled nuggets\nThe whole bag. Split it, or don’t.\nBuilt from their ingredient numbers50095 / 5 / 1376%5.3\nSpicy grilled filet, no bun\nSame move as the regular filet. Spice is free.\nCommon tracker listing14025 / 3 / 471%3.6\nGrilled chicken, no bun\nFilet in a tray or on lettuce.\nCommon tracker listing13023 / 4 / 471%2.9\nEgg White Grill, no muffin\nBreakfast. English muffin in the trash. Black coffee.\nBuilt from their ingredient numbers18026 / 5 / 758%2.2\nGrilled chicken sandwich\nWith the bun. Middle of the pack — still 29% protein.\nTheir nutrition page39028 / 45 / 1129%0.5\n01 — Chick-fil-A\n02 — Chipotle\n03 — Culver’s\n04 — McDonald’s\n05 — Sonic\n06 — Wendy’s\n07 — Taco Bell\n08 — Arby’s\n09 — Burger King\n10 — Subway\n11 — Jimmy John’s\n12 — Domino’s\n13 — Pizza Hut\n14 — Wingstop\n15 — Blaze Pizza\n16 — Starbucks\nFine print\nNothing on this list is under 25% calories from protein. × 10 (40%) is the ideal. 30% or more is still fine.\nP:E is Dr. Ted Naiman’s protein-to-energy ratio: protein grams ÷ (carb grams + fat grams). Higher is better. Green is the good end. Red is the leftover-calories end.\nThese numbers are roughly based on nutrition facts from restaurant websites. Take them with a grain of salt.\nIf there are no available restaurants and you’re stuck with a hotel breakfast, a gas station, an airport — same rules still apply. Eggs, Greek yogurt, string cheese, a "
