   "url": "https://theosismedical.com/hacks/fasting",
   "title": "Intermittent Fasting Hacks",
   "kind": "hack",
   "text": "09\nIntermittent Fasting Hacks\nDifferent clocks. Same idea: a window you can actually keep.\nAnother tool in the toolbelt of trying to live a healthful life in the midst of a chaotic one, is fasting. Contrary to what you might think, I have found this to be the easiest hack to adopt and maintain for over 15 years.\nAt first it may seem a bit tough if you are used to eating all throughout the day, but even just starting by skipping breakfast and eating lunch as your first meal will improve your metabolic flexibility.\nWhat is metabolic flexibility you might ask? This is the ability to seamlessly switch to fat burning when we haven't eaten in a while and to go back and forth between fat and carbs for fuel without feeling hangry or fatigued.\nIntermittent fasting is not one diet. It is a family of eating windows. 16:8, 18:6, OMAD, 5:2, alternate-day — they all shrink the hours (or the days) you eat. The style is a tool. The deficit is still the point.\nI am going to walk through the main styles here so you can see what they actually look like on a Tuesday, not just on an infographic. Pick the one that fits a shift, a hotel breakfast, or a house full of kids. If it does not fit real life, it is not your hack.\nProtein still comes first inside the window. Water, coffee, unsweetened tea outside it. This is not a medical protocol, and it is not for everyone — pregnancy, a history of disordered eating, some diabetes meds, and a few other situations mean you talk to your own clinician first.\nSimplified physiology. Sources: Goodpaster & Sparks, Cell Metabolism (2017) · Smith et al., Endocrine Reviews (2018)\nSources: PMID 29754952 · PMID 28459931 · PMID 38429390 · NIH News in Health\nThe styles\nThis is the menu of styles. Each one will get its own write-up — what a Tuesday looks like, who it actually fits, and how to keep protein from falling off a cliff. For now, pick the clock. The rest is coming.\n16:8\n16 hours off · 8 hours on\nThe default. Skip breakfast or skip late night. Eat in an eight-hour window.\n18:6\n18 hours off · 6 hours on\nTighter window. Two meals, maybe a snack. Still a Tuesday, not a monastery.\n20:4\n20 hours off · 4 hours on\nOne long gap, one short eating block. Easy to under-eat protein if you are not paying attention.\nOMAD\nOne meal a day\nThe whole day’s food in one sitting. High protein still has to fit on that plate.\n5:2\nFive normal days · two low-calorie days\nYou do not shrink every day’s window. Two days a week you eat a lot less.\nAlternate-day\nEat · fast · eat · fast\nEvery other day is the fast. The eat days still need protein and a plan.\nEat-stop-eat\nOne 24-hour fast, 1–2× a week\nDinner to dinner. The other days look normal.\nCircadian / early window\nEat earlier, stop earlier\nPush the window toward daylight. Finish dinner, then stop.\nPSMF\nProtein-sparing modified fast\nNot a clock. A short, aggressive fat-loss phase: high protein, very low carb, very low added fat. Example below.\nCredit where it is due: 16:8 comes from Lean Gains. Eat Stop Eat "
